  ],

  "verdict": "The refreshed Model 3 Long Range addresses most of the outgoing car's complaints — the ride is better, interior materials are improved, and range is up. But the minimalist interior and Tesla's insistence on removing physical stalks remain polarizing. If the range and Supercharger network matter to you, nothing else at this price is close.",
  "pros": "358-mile range, Supercharger access, strong acceleration, refined ride after refresh, lowest cost-per-mile in segment",
  "cons": "No turn-signal stalk, touchscreen-only climate controls, build quality still inconsistent, divisive interior minimalism",

  "bodyText": "Tesla's 2025 Model 3 refresh — codenamed Highland — brings meaningful improvements without changing the fundamental character of the car. The Long Range AWD version now delivers 358 miles of EPA-rated range, up from 333 miles in the outgoing model, thanks to a combination of aerodynamic refinements and a more efficient battery pack. Acceleration remains one of the Model 3's defining traits. Zero to 60 mph happens in 4.2 seconds in the Long Range, which feels quicker than the numbers suggest because the torque arrives instantly. The revised suspension is the biggest functional change. Previous Model 3s rode harshly on anything but smooth pavement. The refresh adds frequency-selective dampers that do a noticeably better job absorbing broken surfaces. Interior improvements focus on material quality. Soft-touch surfaces now cover more of the dashboard and door panels. The ambient lighting strip running the width of the dashboard adds warmth the outgoing car lacked. The 15.4-inch center touchscreen is the only interface for climate, navigation, media, and most vehicle settings. Tesla has doubled down on the touchscreen-only approach by removing the turn-signal stalk — indicators now live on the steering wheel as capacitive buttons. This is the most controversial change and will divide buyers. On the Supercharger network, the Model 3 remains unmatched. Tesla's DC fast charging stations outnumber every other charging network combined, and the integration with the car's software is seamless. Charging curves are strong: 10 to 80 percent takes about 27 minutes at a V3 Supercharger. At $48,990 the Long Range AWD sits in an increasingly crowded segment. But no competitor matches its combination of range, charging network, and software ecosystem.",

  "sections": [
    {
      "sectionTitle": "Range and charging",
      "content": "EPA-rated range is 358 miles. Real-world highway range is closer to 310 miles at 75 mph. Supercharger V3 stations deliver a 10-80% charge in approximately 27 minutes. Home charging on a 240V outlet adds about 30 miles of range per hour. The Tesla Supercharger network includes over 2,500 stations in North America, significantly more than competing networks. Recent opening of Supercharger access to third-party EVs via NACS adapter does not impact charging speed for Tesla vehicles.",
      "orderIndex": 1
    },
    {
      "sectionTitle": "Interior",
      "content": "The refreshed interior uses higher-quality materials throughout. The steering wheel is wrapped in perforated synthetic leather. Door panels have fabric inserts. The 15.4-inch touchscreen is responsive and the software receives regular over-the-air updates. Storage is excellent — the frunk, trunk, and sub-trunk provide more usable space than the exterior suggests. The elimination of the turn-signal stalk is the most controversial change and requires approximately two weeks to adapt to.",
      "orderIndex": 2
    }
  ],

