  "bodyText": "Fuel economy leadership in 2025 belongs almost entirely to hybrids. Our rankings focus on vehicles real buyers actually purchase — no plug-in hybrids, no EVs, no obscure subcompacts that nobody buys. The Toyota Camry XSE Hybrid sits at the top with 52 combined mpg (51 city, 53 highway). That number would have been astonishing a decade ago and remains exceptional today. Toyota's fifth-generation hybrid system pairs a 2.5-liter Atkinson-cycle four-cylinder with two electric motors and an eCVT transmission. Total system output is 232 horsepower. The Honda CR-V Sport Hybrid delivers 40 combined mpg (43 city, 36 highway) in a compact SUV that seats five comfortably. Honda's two-motor hybrid system operates differently than Toyota's — most of the time the gas engine acts as a generator powering the electric motors that drive the wheels. It feels smoother in city driving than the Toyota system. Annual fuel cost estimates assume 15,000 miles driven per year at $3.50 per gallon. The Camry Hybrid costs approximately $1,010 per year to fuel. The CR-V Hybrid costs approximately $1,310 per year. For comparison, a non-hybrid compact SUV like the base CR-V costs approximately $1,640 per year. Over five years of ownership the fuel savings of the hybrid CR-V versus the gasoline version is $1,650. The hybrid premium is roughly $2,800, meaning the hybrid breaks even at approximately 8.5 years of ownership at current gas prices. If gas prices rise or you drive more than 15,000 miles annually, the break-even point moves closer.",

