  "bodyText": "Family SUV buyers in 2025 face an embarrassment of riches. Nearly every mainstream brand has a capable compact or midsize SUV priced under $40,000, and fuel-sipping hybrid options now dominate the category. Our buyer's guide focuses on the vehicles that balance purchase price, running costs, and practicality — the variables that actually matter once the excitement of a new car fades. The Honda CR-V Sport Hybrid leads our overall picks at $34,995 starting price. At 40 combined mpg it leads the segment in fuel economy, and Honda's real-world reliability data places it among the top three most dependable compact SUVs over a five-year ownership period. The Toyota RAV4 Hybrid is a very close second at 39 combined mpg and starts at $33,050. Its TRD Off-Road trim adds genuine capability that the CR-V cannot match if you anticipate actually using the SUV for light off-pavement adventures. The Hyundai Tucson Hybrid rounds out our top three at $34,510. Its strengths are tech features — the largest standard touchscreen in the segment and better in-cabin connectivity than the Honda or Toyota. If you prioritize technology over reliability history, it's worth considering. All three hybrids cost approximately $1,300 per year to fuel at 15,000 annual miles. A non-hybrid compact SUV in the same price range costs approximately $1,650 per year — a $350 per year difference, or $1,750 over five years. That's before accounting for hybrid tax incentives in some states. For buyers prioritizing lower purchase price over long-term fuel savings, the non-hybrid Honda CR-V EX starts at $31,495 and delivers 30 combined mpg, which is still respectable for the class.",

