  "verdict": "The Corvette Z06 delivers supercar performance at a fraction of supercar pricing. Its 670-horsepower naturally aspirated flat-plane-crank V8 is a masterpiece, and the car's handling rivals cars costing three times as much. This is the best performance-per-dollar on sale in 2025.",
  "pros": "Exotic-car performance, extraordinary engine note, relatively attainable pricing, better everyday usability than a Porsche 911 GT3",
  "cons": "Interior materials trail the price point's European competitors, ride harsh even in tour mode, visibility compromised by low seating position",

  "bodyText": "The Chevrolet Corvette Z06 represents something genuinely rare — a naturally aspirated supercar in 2025. While the rest of the performance world has embraced turbocharging, hybridization, and in many cases full electrification, Chevrolet has built the Z06 around a 5.5-liter flat-plane-crank V8 that revs to 8,600 rpm and produces 670 horsepower without any forced induction. On our test track the Z06 posted a lap time of 1:24.8 — faster than the Porsche 911 GT3 at $240,000 and within striking distance of the Porsche 911 Turbo S. Zero to 60 mph takes 2.6 seconds. The quarter mile passes in 10.5 seconds at 131 mph. These are genuinely exotic-car numbers from a $115,000 Chevrolet. The engine is the star of the show. The LT6 flat-plane V8 produces a sound closer to a Ferrari 458 than any previous American V8. Redline is 8,600 rpm and the engine pulls hard all the way there. It's a naturally aspirated experience that is disappearing from the market, and the Corvette Z06 may be the last affordable example of the breed. Handling is remarkable. The mid-engine layout places the powertrain behind the driver for ideal weight distribution — 40 percent front, 60 percent rear. Magnetic ride control suspension keeps the body remarkably flat through fast corners while delivering surprising compliance over imperfect pavement. The Corvette Z06's interior is its weakest aspect. Materials and design are several steps behind Porsche and Ferrari at the same price point. The infotainment system is functional but unremarkable.",

