  "bodyText": "The question of whether to buy an electric vehicle or a hybrid in 2025 ultimately comes down to math — and the math has shifted significantly over the last two years. Our analysis compares two segment-defining vehicles: the 2025 Tesla Model 3 Long Range AWD at $48,990 and the 2025 Toyota Camry XSE Hybrid at $34,995. On fuel and energy costs alone, the Tesla wins decisively. At home charging rates averaging $0.14 per kWh nationally, the Model 3 costs approximately $580 per year to fuel at 15,000 miles. The Camry Hybrid at 52 combined mpg and $3.50 per gallon costs approximately $1,010 per year. That's a $430 per year advantage for the Tesla, or $2,150 over five years. Maintenance favors the Tesla further. EVs have no oil changes, no spark plugs, no timing belts. Brake wear is reduced through regenerative braking — Tesla owners frequently report brake pads lasting 100,000 miles versus 50,000 for most hybrids. Cabin filter and tire rotation are essentially the complete maintenance schedule. Total five-year maintenance for the Model 3 averages $1,200. For the Camry Hybrid it's approximately $3,150. Insurance is where EVs fall behind. The Tesla Model 3 averages $2,150 per year to insure. The Camry Hybrid averages $1,550. Over five years that's $3,000 extra for the Tesla. Depreciation is the largest single cost of new car ownership and the hardest to predict. Tesla depreciation patterns have stabilized significantly since 2023, with current Model 3s retaining approximately 51% of MSRP after three years. Camry Hybrids retain approximately 64% — one of the best depreciation curves in the industry. Combining all running costs, the Tesla saves $4,200 over five years in operating expenses. But its higher purchase price and worse depreciation mean the total cost of ownership still favors the Camry through year eight for the average driver.",

